---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import { TIERS, TIER_ORDER } from '../lib/tiers';

const protocols = (await getCollection('protocols')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const native = protocols.filter((p) => p.data.verification !== 'gateway-mode');
const releases = (await getCollection('releases'))
  .filter((r) => r.data.product === 'gateway')
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.getTime() - a.data.date.getTime() || b.data.version.localeCompare(a.data.version, undefined, { numeric: true }));
const latest = releases[0];

const gate = [
  ['Writes are enabled.', 'safety.allowWrites is off by default, and has been since 4.4.0.'],
  ['Agent writes are enabled.', 'safety.allowMcpWrites is a narrowing of the master switch, not a way around it.'],
  ['The caller has permission.', 'values.write for a logged-in user, which a viewer does not have, or the MCP write setting for an agent.'],
  ['The tag is marked writable.', 'Tags are read-only until somebody decides otherwise, one at a time. A browse import never arms anything.'],
  ['The value is within range.', 'It is rejected, not clamped, because sending 400 when someone asked for 500 is quietly doing something else.'],
  ['The attempt is audited.', 'Before the value reaches the wire, including every refusal, with the actor and the source.'],
];
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Gateway: OPC UA, Modbus and MQTT gateway"
  description="An industrial automation gateway with seven native protocols, ISA-18.2 alarms, a historian, and one write gate that nothing can route around."
  ogAlt="HotLoop Gateway, an industrial automation gateway."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: 'HotLoop Gateway',
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/gateway/',
      description:
        'An industrial automation gateway that polls equipment over OPC UA, Modbus, MQTT and Sparkplug B, EtherNet/IP, S7comm, MTConnect, and HTTP, keeps the history, raises ISA-18.2 alarms, and exposes the plant to agents over MCP.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <span class="label">HotLoop Gateway</span>
      <h1>An industrial gateway that tells you what it has not proven.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        It polls real equipment over real protocols, keeps what it reads, alarms on it, automates against it, and
        exposes the whole plant to agents over MCP. One static Go binary, no sidecar, running as a non-root user
        with every capability dropped.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>Not yet published.</strong>
          <p>
            Version {latest.data.version} is merged. The code has moved into the HotLoop-io org, at
            HotLoop-io/hotloop, and that repository is private for now. Entities, the plant tree, and paging over
            ntfy, Gotify and Discord are merged after {latest.data.version} and don't have a version number yet.
            Nothing here is downloadable yet, and the <a href="/releases/gateway/">release notes</a> say exactly
            which versions have and have not been tagged.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/integrations/">See what it speaks</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/license/">Read the license</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">The write gate</span>
        <h2>Every write goes through one function that cannot be routed around.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          From the UI, the API, an automation, or an agent. Policy lives in that one place rather than being
          re-implemented at each call site, where one of them would eventually be forgotten.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="gate">
        {gate.map(([head, body], i) => (
          <li class="card reveal">
            <span class="step mono">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div><h3>{head}</h3><p>{body}</p></div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="prose muted after">
        An MCP client gets no special path. <code>write_tag</code> hits the same gate an operator does, and it
        requires a stated reason that is recorded next to the write, because an audit row nobody can interpret six
        months later is barely better than none.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Built for agents</span>
        <h2>MCP in both directions.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          16 tools and 4 resources, over streamable HTTP and over stdio, so an agent can read the plant and,
          only through the gate, act on it. An agent can work in entities, the infeed pump rather than
          register 40001, and <code>call_service</code> crosses the same gate <code>write_tag</code> does. There is also an outbound client, so a rule can call a tool on
          another system.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          The Sites screen uses the same mechanism. Point one gateway at the others as outbound MCP servers and it
          asks each of them the question it would answer itself. A site that does not answer is shown as
          unreachable, with the reason, and is left out of the roll-up, because counting a plant you cannot reach
          as zero alarms would be the worst possible default.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Bad is not zero</span>
        <h2>A failed read is not a value.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Every reading carries good, uncertain, or bad. A driver that cannot read a point still emits a reading,
          marked bad, because silence looks identical to a value that has not changed.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          Alarm evaluation holds its previous state on a bad reading rather than evaluating it. A sensor that
          fails to zero would otherwise trip every low alarm on the unit and look like a process event, when it is
          an instrument problem.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What it speaks</span>
        <h2>Seven native protocols, and a table that says how far each has been proven.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-4">
        {native.map((p) => (
          <a class="card proto reveal" href={`/integrations/${p.id}/`}>
            <div class="cluster head">
              <h3>{p.data.name}</h3>
              <span class={`chip ${TIERS[p.data.verification].chip}`}>{TIERS[p.data.verification].label}</span>
            </div>
            <p>{p.data.summary}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="notice after">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>S7comm and EtherNet/IP have never been run against a physical PLC.</strong>
          <p>
            Their decoding is tested thoroughly, but tested decoding is not the same as a value that came off a
            real controller. If you are the first to point either at real hardware, treat it as commissioning and
            check a known value before you trust a screen.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What is inside</span>
        <h2>The rest of the plant, in one binary.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Historian</h3><p>TimescaleDB, promoted to a hypertable when the extension is there and falling back to native range partitioning when it is not. Per-tag deadband filtering, so a sensor jittering in its last bit does not write a row every scan forever.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Alarms</h3><p>ISA-18.2, including <code>rtn-unack</code> for an alarm that cleared before anybody saw it. Acknowledgement and return-to-normal are independent axes, so a transient trip at 03:00 is still on the list when the morning shift arrives.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Automations</h3><p>Nine trigger types, a sandboxed condition, and actions. Conditions are compiled when a rule is saved, so a broken rule is caught in the editor and not at 3am.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The Board</h3><p>The screen that gets left on a wall. Pinned tags are gauges with their alarm limits marked where they fall, layout stored with the plant so the panel PC and the laptop show the same board.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Entities</h3><p>The plant the way people talk about it: the infeed pump, not register 40001. Every tag gets an entity on its own, and a motor, a valve or a PID loop is one entity built from the tags it really is. Commands go through the write gate like everything else.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Plant tree</h3><p>One ISA-95 equipment tree, enterprise down to equipment, plus labels. Devices and entities hang off it, and the site, area and line a device already carried stay in step with it.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Paging</h3><p>ntfy, Gotify, Discord, webhook and email, so a 03:00 alarm reaches a phone. ntfy's Acknowledge button and the Discord bot's Acknowledge and Shelve buttons act on the alarm itself, through signed, single-use links. Discord has only been run against a stand-in, not the real thing. Rate limited, retried, and off by default.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Reports</h3><p>Historian numbers on a schedule, as CSV or HTML, stored and optionally emailed. Only good readings are counted, each row says how many it left out, and a period with no readings says no data, never zero.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>OPC UA server</h3><p>Read-only, so a third-party client can browse and read the Gateway's tags. Secure channels, the same users as the web login, no write handler at all, and no holes in the address space while somebody edits a device.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Edge Relay</h3><p>A second, lighter binary with no database, about 16.9 MB against the Gateway's 33.4 MB, that polls and forwards over Sparkplug B. Fleet management tracks and commands registered relays.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Embedded broker</h3><p>An optional in-process MQTT broker, so a single-box install can be its own Sparkplug hub. There is no per-topic access control by design, so use a dedicated broker at scale.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Backups</h3><p>A logical snapshot taken in pure Go over the existing connection, with a restore that refuses to touch a database that already has data. It loads an older backup by column name, and refuses one from a newer version outright.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free for individuals. Free for business, through Embernet.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            The Gateway is source-available under the HotLoop Community License, which is not an OSI open source
            license. Individual, home, hobbyist, nonprofit, and educational use is free. Any business or for-profit
            use goes through <a href="https://embernet.ai">Embernet</a>, which provides the support and the warranty.
            You can sign up for Embernet for free, and business use of HotLoop there is free. If you want a company to
            design, install, and support the rollout for you, <a href="https://fireballz.ai">Fireball Industries</a> is
            our official systems integrator.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the terms</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }

  ol.gate { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s3); counter-reset: none; }
  ol.gate li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; gap: var(--hl-s5); align-items: start; padding: var(--hl-s5) var(--hl-s6); }
  ol.gate .step { font-size: var(--hl-fs-xl); font-weight: 800; color: var(--hl-ink-faint); line-height: 1; padding-top: 2px; }
  ol.gate h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-md); margin-bottom: var(--hl-s1); }
  .after { margin-top: var(--hl-s6); }

  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .proto { text-decoration: none; color: inherit; display: block; transition: transform var(--hl-transition-fast), border-color var(--hl-transition-fast); }
  .proto:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); border-color: var(--hl-orange); }
  .proto .head { justify-content: space-between; align-items: start; margin-bottom: var(--hl-s2); }
  .proto h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-md); margin: 0; }

  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
